An Abstract Data Type (ADT) is a high-level description of a data structure that defines what operations can be performed on the data, but hides how those operations are implemented.
In C, we implement an ADT by separating the interface (the .h file) from the implementation (the .c file). This is exactly what we did with the Bank Account example, but let’s look at it through the lens of a Stack (a classic ADT).


